// endpoint_deploy.proto — Deploy: put the checkout's current source into
// service. DAEMON ADMIN section.
//
// THE DAEMON OWNS DEPLOYS (owner design, 2026-09-23). There is no deploy
// script. A caller asks — the `claude-repld deploy` CLI verb, Emacs's
// `agent-repl-deploy`, or the daemon itself once per COMPLETE change landing on
// master through its own merge (one landing, one deploy, never one per
// commit) — and the daemon does the rest:
//
//   1. BUILD every component — protobufs, the shim bundle, the webapp, the
//      daemon, the store and the sidecar — into a STAGING location. A build
//      failure deploys NOTHING: no artifact is installed and nothing is
//      restarted, and the failure is this call's `build_failed` answer.
//   2. DECIDE WHAT IS OUT OF DATE BY CONTENT HASH: each running process's
//      reported build (every process reports it when it connects) against the
//      freshly built one. Never "did this build change the file".
//   3. INSTALL the staged artifacts, then restart exactly what is out of date,
//      each WHEN it may:
//        - store and sidecar at once, in the recorded safe order (they hold no
//          turn state);
//        - an out-of-date shim with nothing in flight at once; one with a turn
//          or detached work in flight is REGISTERED and bounced when that work
//          ends. Queued prompts never block a bounce: they are delivered to the
//          new shim;
//        - an out-of-date daemon by the blue-green handover, whose successor
//          adopts the shims and bounces the stale ones the same way;
//        - elisp and the webapp by a pushed reload (WatchDaemon's
//          `reload_elisp`, the host stream's `reload_webapp`).
//
// AN UNFORCED DEPLOY ENDS NO TURN. That is the whole difference from
// UpdateShutdownSchedule{now}, the operator's emergency stop. `force` is the
// one way to not wait.
//
// THE ANSWER IS THE DEPLOY'S DECISIONS, NOT ITS COMPLETION. A registered
// bounce waits on its workspace's work for as long as that takes, so
// completion has no bound this call could hold open. What follows arrives on
